GeneID:57172, calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase
Calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase (CaMK) is a family of serine/threonine protein kinases activated by the binding of calcium and calmodulin. In humans, the CaMK family includes several isoforms, with CaMKII, CaMKIV, and CaMKI being the most well-known. These kinases play a crucial role in various cellular processes, such as signal transduction, synaptic plasticity, learning, memory, and muscle contraction. They are involved in regulating the activity of other proteins through phosphorylation in response to calcium signaling. Dysregulation of CaMKs has been implicated in various diseases, including neurodegenerative disorders, heart disease, and cancer. Their activity is tightly controlled and influenced by cellular calcium levels.

