GeneID:2328 flavin containing dimethylaniline monoxygenase 3
Flavin-containing dimethylaniline monoxygenase 3 (FMO3) is a gene in humans that encodes an enzyme involved in the metabolism of xenobiotics and endogenous compounds. It is part of the flavin-containing monooxygenase (FMO) family, which catalyzes the oxidation of various substrates, including drugs, environmental toxins, and endogenous amines. FMO3 is primarily expressed in the liver and plays a key role in the detoxification process, particularly in the oxidation of tertiary amines. Mutations in the FMO3 gene are associated with a condition called trimethylaminuria (also known as fish odor syndrome), where the body cannot properly metabolize trimethylamine, leading to a strong body odor. FMO3 also influences the metabolism of several drugs and dietary components.

