To sum up, we oserved that genes involved in development processes, basic cell functions like transcription or translation and cell structure seem to be more conserved between species, which mean they had more pressure to maintain their functions during the evolution process. On the other side, genes involved in immunosystem processes have less pressure to maintain the same functions and are more likely to appear a variance. Finally, we showed that two genes (Wnt family member 2B, E2F transcription factor) related to cell proliferation and cancer progression tent to have the largest diversity among the genes we study and that can't explained so easily and from my prespective it deserve some more deep research on how this genes diverse among species and how this impact the function of the corresponding proteins.

On the other hand, as far as concerned the exon trees, we don't observed a certain pattern among the genes, the more concerve exons belong to and also, that genes are not actually so concerve as they are not matched with the genes we found as concerve from the gene trees. Maybe, how concerve an exon is between species doesn't have to do, with how concerve the gene is, but with important is this specific exon in the protein structure and function. For, example if an exon contain the sequence for an enzyme active domain of a protein and another exon of the same gene contain the sequence for an internal structure with no active domain, we expect the first exon to maintain less diversity, due to evolution pressure.  